(* README *)

1 - Summary
    -------

    L003 formalizes the SOLVER'S PARADOX: a diagonal obstruction to
    certification in any language with quotation, pairing, negation,
    duplication, and existential witness projection. For every verifier `v`
    the formation rules build the explicit diagonal sentence

      g_v = neg (dup (proj v)),

    and the claim that `g_v` holds of its own quotation is equivalent to its
    non-acceptance by `v`. Where acceptance is decided, `v` either accepts a
    false claim or leaves a true claim uncertified:

      - `coding_free_identity`      : at every datum, by formation laws only
      - `certification_fixed_point` : claim  <->  ~ acceptance
      - `certification_dichotomy`   : decided acceptance  <->  dichotomy
      - `dichotomy_sharpness`       : dichotomy  <->  excluded middle
      - `brute_force_evaluates`     : `BF_n` evaluates level `n` internally
      - `evaluator_quadratic_lower_bound` 
                                    : every evaluator of level `n`
                                      has level at least `(n - 4) (n + 2)`
      - `solvers_paradox_plain_sat` : the plain-SAT specialization

    The fixed point assumes neither soundness, completeness, nor
    decidability. The concrete bit-stream language, relativized to an
    arbitrary oracle and graded by serialization length, contains a
    brute-force evaluator `BF_n` for every level; the certifiers read off from
    it form a bounded certification family, so the ascent hierarchy holds
    there unconditionally. Every circuit verifier determines a plain
    propositional formula `psi_v`, no larger than `v`, which truth tables
    classify correctly, while `v` fails to certify the associated diagonal
    claim.

    The construction is valid at one level, self-referential only under a
    coding, and settled one level up. The underlying identity holds at every
    datum and uses no property of quotation; the obstruction holds under
    every quotation, but which branch obtains depends on the coding; and the
    diagonal claim is decided by any internal evaluator whose domain contains
    `g_v`, which lies strictly above `v`.

    `L003_CORE_CONTRACT` contains the coding-free identity, the dichotomy,
    the size-free fixed point, brute-force evaluation, and the Solver's
    Paradox for plain SAT. `L003_CONTRACT` collects all thirty-eight
    contracts.


2 - Reading Contract
    ----------------

    L003 concerns certification, not classification. Every finite
    propositional formula is decided by `classify`, and
    `TRUTH_TABLE_CONTRACT` proves this. No contract states that a SAT
    classifier misclassifies an instance, and nothing in L003 concerns the
    complexity of satisfiability or the relation of P and NP.

    Semantics is proposition-valued in every layer. `Accepts C v e x` means
    that some witness makes `v` hold of the encoded claim; it is acceptance,
    not truth. Quotation carries no abstract law; the concrete serialization
    is proved to parse uniquely.

    `CODING_FREE_IDENTITY_CONTRACT` is a statement about one verifier at one
    datum. Reading it as a claim that `g_v` is about itself requires setting
    the datum to the quotation of `g_v` and reading the verifier's input as
    a claim. `CODING_SENSITIVITY_CONTRACT` shows that this reading is
    coding-relative: the fixed point holds under every quotation map, while
    the branch of `TBit 0` flips between the standard quotation and its
    bitwise negation, both injective. Faithful interpretations commute with
    quotation and so cannot perform such a recoding.

    The Certification Dichotomy is indexed by decidability of acceptance at
    the single diagonal claim. `DICHOTOMY_SHARPNESS_CONTRACT` shows that the
    dichotomy for all verifiers complete at their claim is equivalent to
    excluded middle, so the premise cannot be removed intuitionistically.
    Decidable semantics, in particular Boolean semantics, supplies it for
    every verifier; truth tables supply it for every circuit verifier. The
    dichotomy does not say that the diagonal claim is unknowable or that
    another verifier cannot certify it; changing the verifier changes the
    diagonal sentence.

    `ASCENT_HIERARCHY_CONTRACT`, `ASCENT_BOUNDS_CONTRACT`, and
    `PROLIFERATION_CONTRACT` take a `BoundedCertificationFamily`.
    `BRUTE_FORCE_EVALUATION_CONTRACT` constructs one for the concrete
    language, and `CONCRETE_HIERARCHY_CONTRACT` is the resulting
    unconditional instance. The hierarchy states that each certifier is not
    correct at its own diagonal claim; the sharper dichotomy branch follows
    wherever acceptance there is decided.

    Diagonal-closed levels are empty whenever negation and duplication
    strictly raise levels, as they do under serialization length.
    Statements phrased through diagonal-closed levels are then vacuous; the
    operative bound is `n < lvl u + c_dup + c_neg` for every evaluator `u`
    of level `n`.

    `FaithfulInterpretation` and `SizeFreeInterpretation` preserve the
    diagonal claim and non-acceptance. Acceptance itself is preserved where
    it is decided on both sides: without decidability, `~ A <-> ~ B` does not
    give `A <-> B`.

    Relativization is a theorem about one syntax with oracle-dependent
    semantics: quotation, formation, `BF_n`, the certifiers, and the diagonal
    sentences are the same terms for every oracle, and the fixed point and
    the bounded family hold uniformly. L003 contains no complexity theory;
    the consequence that the framework cannot by itself separate P from NP
    remains metatheoretic.

    Internal certification is a separate, syntactic layer. `HilbertKS` and
    `DerivabilityConditions` are explicit premises of every theorem that uses
    them, and `pc_prov` is not related to semantic truth. The language-level
    fixed point uses K/S, box representation, and the certified negation and
    duplication rules, but not D1-D3; its Gödel II consequence uses D1-D3.


3 - State
    -----

                      ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
                      │                  x               │
                      │   └─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┘ 8 / 10   │
                      └──────────────────────────────────┘

    Evaluation: 8, Solid. Mechanized, documented, and extracted; reduced to
    thirty-eight explicit contracts with closed assumption reports. The
    bounded family and the hierarchy are constructed in the concrete
    language, every internal evaluator is bounded below quadratically,
    Tseitin conversion is certified, the SAT layer relativizes, and the
    internal premises have a model. D1-D3 for a concrete calculus remain
    open. Not peer reviewed.


4 - Intuition
    ---------

    The abstract shape is Lawvere's: a point-surjective `A -> (A -> bool)`
    would give Boolean negation a fixed point. L003 realizes this inside a
    language, with rows as expressions, columns as data, and quotation as the
    diagonal input. Duplication copies the input and negation negates:

      [[ d_u ]](y)  <->  ~ [[ u ]](<y, y>),     d_u = neg (dup u).

    At `y = code d_u`, `u` and `d_u` cannot agree, because no proposition is
    equivalent to its own negation. If `u` evaluated a level containing
    `d_u` they would agree, so evaluators ascend: an evaluator of level `n`
    has level above `n` minus the formation cost.

    Projection turns a verifier into its acceptance predicate:

      [[ proj v ]](z)  <->  exists w, [[ v ]](<z, wit w>).

    Applying the same construction to `proj v` gives `g_v`:

                       v
                       │  proj
                       ▼
           acceptance of claims  ── dup ──▶ claim about itself
                                                    │
                                                    │  neg
                                                    ▼
                          g_v at code g_v  <->  ~ (v accepts it)

    Deciding acceptance yields exactly one branch:

      (i)   v accepts the diagonal claim,  and the claim is false;
      (ii)  v does not accept it,          and the claim is true.

    The verifier `TConst true` realizes (i); `TConst false` realizes (ii).
    Without a decision the branches cannot be separated: a base expression
    meaning `Q \/ ~ Q` makes the dichotomy equivalent to that disjunction.

    In the concrete language, data are bit streams and pairing interleaves
    even and odd positions. `BF_n` is the disjunction, over every term `e` of
    level at most `n`, of

      (even bits spell the serialization of e)  /\  reindex (e, odd).

    Unique parsing makes exactly one disjunct fire on `<code e, x>`, and
    reindexing makes it read `x`; duplication is absorbed into the index
    map, and projection keeps its node with a shifted map. Reading only the
    claim half of a certification datum turns `BF_n` into a certifier `V n`.

    Fix a circuit verifier `v`: at the diagonal claim, even positions are
    constants from the quotation of `g_v`, and odd positions are witness
    bits. Replacing witness bits by variables gives

      ConcreteAccepted O v  <->  Satisfiable psi_v,     |psi_v| <= |v|.

    Truth tables decide `psi_v`, so the branch is decided, but in neither
    branch do acceptance and truth agree. Tseitin conversion moves `psi_v`
    into CNF with at most `3 |v| + 1` clauses of width three.

    Bounded certifiers do not escape. If `V n` is sound and complete up to
    level `n`, its diagonal sentence lies strictly above `n`. A certifier for
    that higher level settles the old claim and creates a new one:

      n_0  <  lvl (g (V n_0))  =  n_1  <  lvl (g (V n_1))  =  n_2  <  ...

    with `n_(k+1) = lvl (V n_k) + 9` in the concrete language. What grows is
    the certification boundary, not one unresolved sentence.


